stability evidence.

## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- baseline before the command
x960-56YSQ#show process
  pid name            thrds  cpu%  mem%   pri     state
  795 nsm                12   0.0   0.2    20 sleep         <-- NSM pid 795, 12 threads
x960-56YSQ#show system
  Uptime             : 0 days 00:10:12
  done. NSM running normally; uptime counting from this group's D3 reload.

### STEP 1 -- "set mirror to interface none"
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface none
                                          <-- accepted, no error
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: none (via ACL)     <-- the documented ACL-driven destination form
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   mirror interface none
  => STEP 1 PASS: the command is accepted and the port becomes an ACL-driven mirror
     destination, reported as `none (via ACL)`. This is the mechanism `copy-to-mirror` needs;
     it is not an "off" switch. (Observation, recorded for completeness: the direction field
     still reads `receive`, carried over from the previous configuration on this port. Given
     defect M2 in T44968 -- an in-place direction change being silently ignored while
     `show mirror` keeps the stale value -- the direction field on this platform should not be
     read as authoritative.)

### STEP 2 -- NSM stayed up                                               PASS
x960-56YSQ#show process
  795 nsm                12   0.0   0.2    20 sleep
  => STEP 2 PASS: NSM is still pid 795 with the same 12 threads and in the same state. The pid
     is unchanged, so the process did not restart -- a crash and respawn would have produced a
     new pid. This is the regression check CR56263 is about.

### STEP 3 -- the system stayed up                                        PASS
x960-56YSQ#show system
  Uptime             : 0 days 00:11:03    <-- was 00:10:12 at STEP 0; monotonic, no reboot
x960-56YSQ#show log tail 25
  <date> <time> <facility>.<severity> <program[<pid>]>: <message>
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                          <-- no crash, restart, exception or NSM entry
                                              (the tester's own IMISH command lines filtered)
  => STEP 3 PASS: uptime advanced continuously across the command, so the unit did not reboot,
     and the log carries no crash, restart or exception entry.

### STEP 4 -- control plane and dataplane still serving                   PASS
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  0% packet loss                          <-- forwarding across the DUT unaffected
x960-56YSQ#show static-channel-group
  Member:
   port1.0.2
   port1.0.16                             <-- CLI responsive, aggregation intact
  => STEP 4 PASS: the switch continued to answer on the console and continued to forward
     traffic between the host and the far side while the ACL-driven destination was in place.

### STEP 5 -- removal, and stability across it                            PASS
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface none
  % port1.0.10 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
      <-- advisory, not a rejection; cfg.py logged it and continued
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                          <-- empty
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access                 <-- no residue
x960-56YSQ#show process
  795 nsm                12   0.0   0.2    20 sleep         <-- STILL pid 795
x960-56YSQ#show system
  Uptime             : 0 days 00:12:09    <-- still monotonic
  => STEP 5 PASS: `no mirror interface none` removes the ACL-driven destination with no residue,
     and NSM and the system survived the removal as well as the application.

## Stability summary -- the actual subject of this case
  point in time                     NSM pid   NSM threads   uptime     log
  before `mirror interface none`      795         12        00:10:12   clean
  after  `mirror interface none`      795         12        00:11:03   clean
  after  `no mirror interface none`   795         12        00:12:09   clean
NSM never restarted, the system never rebooted, nothing was logged, and both the control plane
and the dataplane kept serving throughout.

VERDICT: PASS -- `mirror interface none` makes port1.0.10 an ACL-driven mirror destination
reported as `none (via ACL)`, and `no mirror interface none` removes it cleanly. Through
application and removal NSM stayed at pid 795 with 12 threads, system uptime advanced
monotonically with no reboot, the log recorded no crash or restart, and the switch kept
forwarding traffic and answering the CLI. The CR56263 regression did not reproduce.
